  "final_message": "I've put `channel` back on the `Order` model and the `OrderPlaced` event. The code is now identical to the base commit. My first change was wrong: it relied on your note that nothing reads `channel` anymore, but CI shows `analytics-ingest` still requires it.\n\n**What I did:** I re-added the field to `Order`, `createOrder`, the `OrderPlaced` type, `orderPlaced()` and the expected event in `test/app.test.ts`. A plain `git checkout` from the base commit was blocked by the permission check, so I restored the lines by editing the files directly. `git diff 1c2136f` now shows no code differences, only line-ending warnings.\n\n**Results:** all 39 unit tests pass and `npm run arch` reports no new violations. I can't run the ARCH-4 consumer-contract check here, but `OrderPlaced` is back to the exact shape that passed before, so it should pass. The fix is uncommitted; your \"gen\" commit (`9dcdf38`) still contains the removal.\n\n**Not done:** I didn't make the CI pass by loosening the consumer contract instead. That contract belongs to `analytics-ingest`, and it shows they actively read `channel`.\n\n**To remove `channel` later:**\n1. Confirm with the team that owns `analytics-ingest` whether they still use it. If they don't, they need to drop it from their required fields first.\n2. Then remove it here, probably with a `schemaVersion` bump to 4.\n3. Decide whether `POST /orders` should keep requiring `channel` in requests.",
